# Line endings.
#
# Every text file is stored LF in the repository and checked out LF everywhere.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, checkout behaviour is decided by whatever
# `core.autocrlf` each clone happens to have set. On this machine it is `true`,
# so most of the tree lands CRLF -- but files created fresh by an editor arrive
# LF, and the result is a working tree that mixes both. `kernel/src/scheduler.rs`
# was LF while every other kernel source was CRLF; the fbreclaim crates came out
# LF too. That inconsistency never shows up as a diff, because git normalises to
# LF on the way in either way, which is exactly what makes it easy to accumulate
# and confusing to hit: a byte-oriented edit (a scripted patch, an exact-match
# replacement) behaves differently from file to file for no visible reason, and
# twice corrupted a file into `\r\r\n` line endings before it was noticed.
#
# LF rather than CRLF because that is what is already stored, what CI builds on,
# and what a Linux contributor would expect from a Rust repository. Expect the
# next checkout to rewrite the working-tree endings of most files; that is the
# point, and it produces no commit.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary and generated artifacts: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.img binary
*.patch -text
